---
import SiteShell from "../../app/providers/site-shell.astro";
import Callout from "../../shared/ui/callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../shared/ui/code-block.astro";
import Heading from "../../shared/ui/heading.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../shared/ui/page-header.astro";
import TextLink from "../../shared/ui/text-link.astro";
---

<SiteShell
  title="Usecases"
  description="Practical type-tune workflows for shared types, path aliases, barrels, and generated files."
  withSidebar
>
  <article class="prose">
    <PageHeader
      eyebrow="Usage · Practical cases"
      title="Usecases"
      lead="Choose the smallest safe workflow for the declaration you need to move."
    />

    <div class="card-grid usecases content-section">
      <section class="info-card pixel-frame">
        <h2>A feature type became shared</h2>
        <p>Use scan, plan, review, dry-run, and apply when several files now depend on it.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="info-card pixel-frame">
        <h2>Move one known symbol</h2>
        <p>Use <code>extract</code> when the declaration and destination are already clear.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="info-card pixel-frame">
        <h2>Preserve path aliases</h2>
        <p>Existing <code>baseUrl</code> and <code>paths</code> aliases are kept when they still reach the destination.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="info-card pixel-frame">
        <h2>Keep simple barrels working</h2>
        <p>A simple type re-export can be updated. A riskier barrel keeps the original compatibility re-export.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="info-card pixel-frame">
        <h2>Skip generated sources</h2>
        <p>Generated files are excluded by default. Include them only when the move is intentional.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="info-card pixel-frame">
        <h2>Annotate risky candidates</h2>
        <p>Optional AI review adds notes to the plan without affecting deterministic rewrite logic.</p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="content-section">
      <Heading>Extract one symbol</Heading>
      <p>
        <code>extract</code> moves one exported type or interface even when it has fewer referrers
        than <code>scan</code> would list. The symbol name comes first. <code>--from</code> is the
        file that declares it. Omit <code>--to</code> to use <code>--placement-style</code>, which
        defaults to <code>collocation</code>.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock code={`pnpm exec type-tune extract OrderSummary \\
  --from src/features/order/schema.ts \\
  --to src/features/order/types/order.ts \\
  --project tsconfig.json \\
  --dry-run`} />
      <p>
        The dry run writes the move, runs <code>tsc --noEmit --project</code>, and restores the
        files when that finishes normally. Repeat the command without <code>--dry-run</code> to
        keep it. The destination must be a new project-relative TypeScript source file and must
        differ from <code>--from</code>.
      </p>
      <Callout>
        After npm install, the same command is <code>npx type-tune extract OrderSummary --from ...</code>.
        The full batch workflow is in <TextLink href="/usage/workflow">Workflow</TextLink>.
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <Heading>Path aliases and barrels</Heading>
      <p>
        type-tune reads <code>compilerOptions.baseUrl</code> and <code>compilerOptions.paths</code>.
        When an existing alias still points at the new file, rewritten imports keep that alias.
        Otherwise the import becomes a project-relative specifier.
      </p>
      <p>
        A named type re-export through a simple barrel can be rewritten to the new module. When
        that edit would be unsafe, including a mixed value re-export or a value wildcard while the
        source still has other code, the barrel stays unchanged and the original module keeps a
        type-only compatibility re-export.
      </p>
      <p>
        That re-export lets unscanned Svelte, Astro, JavaScript, and external consumers keep
        importing the old path. It does not typecheck those consumers. Vue components with supported
        TypeScript script blocks are counted, and a direct type-import specifier in those scripts
        can be updated. Generated files stay out of the scan unless you pass
        <code>--include-generated</code>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</SiteShell>

<style>
  .usecases {
    max-width: none;
  }
</style>
